Shared aquaculture lifecycle (@farming-aquaculture-cycle: 0, #part-of @farming-aquatic-foods, #source @farm-source-aquaculture) { r_5SDEDY1HV37R18H68BMPW787WX
1. Confirm the actual species, local permissions, water source, climate, escape prevention, receiving-water capacity, market, and processing route. Do not release non-native farm animals into natural waters.
2. Design ponds, tanks, or cages around biomass, oxygen demand, water treatment, and emergency power/aeration. A tank volume alone does not define a safe stocking density.
3. Buy traceable healthy eggs, fry, juveniles, or postlarvae from an appropriate hatchery. Match temperature and water chemistry during transfer; avoid mixing unassessed stocks.
4. Track temperature, dissolved oxygen, pH, ammonia/nitrite where relevant, feed, mortality, growth, and water quality. Check oxygen during high-risk periods such as before dawn in productive ponds. Feed a suitable ration and remove avoidable waste.
5. Grade only when needed, minimize handling stress, and adjust biomass and feeding to measured growth and water capacity. Veterinary oversight determines disease treatments and withdrawal periods.
6. Harvest at the intended size through a species-appropriate humane method, keep equipment clean, chill promptly, and separate product from dirty pond or processing water.
7. Account for escapes, effluent, sludge, mortalities, feed conversion, and energy. Clean or fallow between groups as the system requires. Breeding and larval production are separate specialist stages unless explicitly included.

Practice: model the oxygen and feed demand at final biomass before stocking anything. [Embrapa family aquaculture manual](https://www.infoteca.cnptia.embrapa.br/infoteca/handle/doc/1167143).
} r_5SDEDY1HV37R18H68BMPW787WX

Nile tilapia — Oreochromis niloticus (@oreochromis-niloticus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-aquatic-foods, #source @farm-source-tilapia) { r_7RH7J4VDPFASGW3T692QJ17V0P
Warm-water freshwater aquaculture example; the generic tilapia food entry does not confirm this species or strain. Use legally appropriate healthy juveniles and a system suited to roughly 25–30°C growth conditions. Stock only to a designed biomass and oxygen capacity, not a universal fish-per-litre rule. Uncontrolled breeding can cause crowding; use an appropriate verified hatchery strategy rather than improvising hormone treatment.

Lifecycle: broodstock, eggs, mouthbrooded fry, nursery, grow-out, harvest. Buying juveniles avoids specialist hatchery work. Feed for size and water conditions, sample growth, and monitor oxygen and nitrogenous waste. Market size often takes several months, depending strongly on starting size, temperature, feed, and system. Harvest humanely, chill promptly, treat effluent, and prevent escape. [FAO Nile tilapia profile](https://www.fao.org/fishery/docs/DOCUMENT/aquaculture/CulturedSpecies/file/en/en_niletilapia.htm).
} r_7RH7J4VDPFASGW3T692QJ17V0P

Atlantic salmon — Salmo salar (@salmo-salar: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-aquatic-foods, #source @farm-source-salmon, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-171998) { r_35GQXWVW87QD89F9KVM8SM6059
The USDA wild Atlantic salmon entry explicitly identifies this species; farmed fish do not inherit its exact nutrient values. This is specialized cold-water production, unsuitable for an ordinary warm garden pond.

Lifecycle: freshwater eggs, alevins using yolk, fry, parr, smolts, grow-out, and mature adults. Smolt transfer and saltwater systems need carefully managed acclimation; some production uses controlled recirculating systems. Maintain high oxygen, appropriate temperature, complete feed, health monitoring, and escape control. Egg-to-market production usually takes years across stages, not a quick warm-water crop. Use specialist hatchery and veterinary protocols, humane harvest, rapid chilling, effluent management, and planned fallowing or cleaning. [FAO Atlantic salmon profile](https://www.fao.org/fishery/docs/DOCUMENT/aquaculture/CulturedSpecies/file/en/en_atlanticsalmon.htm).
} r_35GQXWVW87QD89F9KVM8SM6059

Whiteleg shrimp — Penaeus vannamei (@penaeus-vannamei: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-aquatic-foods, #source @farm-source-shrimp) { r_468E0P5T03HWMSZ7TJK53YP1GB
Also published as Litopenaeus vannamei. This aquaculture example is not a species identification for the TACO Rio Grande shrimp entries, which may represent wild marine shrimp.

Lifecycle: broodstock, eggs, nauplii, zoea, mysis, postlarvae, nursery, and grow-out. Buy appropriately screened postlarvae from a reliable hatchery. Match salinity, temperature, and mineral balance to the designed system; fresh-looking water is not enough. Warm-water grow-out commonly takes several months. Track dissolved oxygen, pH, alkalinity, ammonia, feed trays, growth, moulting, and mortality. Avoid uncontrolled water exchange and untreated discharges. Harvest humanely, chill promptly, and manage sludge and crop breaks. Dense culture requires professional design and reliable aeration. [FAO profile](https://www.fao.org/fishery/docs/DOCUMENT/aquaculture/CulturedSpecies/file/en/en_whitelegshrimp.htm).
} r_468E0P5T03HWMSZ7TJK53YP1GB

Curimbatá — Prochilodus lineatus (@prochilodus-lineatus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-aquatic-foods, #source @farm-source-native-fish) { r_27JNN3SPM2ANDEJ2ZAVPWDR7VA
One curimbatá/corimba identity; Prochilodus species vary by basin and food names do not settle identity. A migratory spawning fish: pond grow-out and hatchery reproduction are different stages. Obtain legally appropriate identified juveniles from a competent hatchery rather than trying to reproduce a river flood cycle in a garden tank.

Use a locally designed warm-water pond system, manage natural food and any supplemental feeding appropriately, and monitor oxygen, biomass, growth, and water quality through the shared cycle. Harvest timing depends on starting size, temperature, and target market; do not transfer tilapia's schedule. Use humane harvest, prompt chilling, and escape prevention. Retain broodstock only within a supported hatchery programme.
} r_27JNN3SPM2ANDEJ2ZAVPWDR7VA

Pintado — Pseudoplatystoma corruscans (@pseudoplatystoma-corruscans: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-aquatic-foods, #source @farm-source-native-fish, #source @farm-source-surubim) { r_2VVZS4NVRVG4DN3AXDNM2CWRA5
One pintado identity; commercial surubim hybrids and related species are not interchangeable. Use verified hatchery juveniles with declared species or hybrid parentage. Carnivorous juveniles need suitable prepared feed and size management; inappropriate mixed sizes and feeding can cause losses.

Lifecycle: specialized broodstock and larval stages, nursery, grow-out, harvest. Plan ponds or tanks around final biomass, oxygen, feed cost, and effluent, with veterinary support. Sample growth and size distribution and control handling stress. Harvest at the intended size using a trained humane process, chill promptly, and keep batches traceable. No fixed household stocking density or harvest date is supplied because these depend on the actual strain and system.
} r_2VVZS4NVRVG4DN3AXDNM2CWRA5

Atlantic sardine — Sardina pilchardus (@sardina-pilchardus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-aquatic-foods, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-175139, #source @farm-source-fisheries, #source @farm-source-sardine) { r_4AJES2KPQTXTB6HVJDEY8VNKJ6
The USDA Atlantic sardine canned-food entry identifies this source more closely than generic Brazilian sardinha. A wild pelagic schooling fish with drifting eggs, plankton-feeding larvae and adults, seasonal movement, and a capture-fishery harvest rather than an ordinary small-farm grow-out cycle.

Use traceable legal landings, prompt chilling, hygienic processing, and validated canning. Canned fish with edible bone has a different nutrient profile from raw fillet or another sardine species. Assess stock-specific season and management through local fisheries authorities; no generic calendar or claim of unlimited sustainable supply follows from this Record.
} r_4AJES2KPQTXTB6HVJDEY8VNKJ6

