| Nutrition name | Identity and production decision |
| --- | --- |
| Abadejo | Several market identities exist; obtain scientific name and origin. Usually a marine capture product, not a garden fish. |
| Atum | Several tuna species. Pelagic capture fisheries and specialized industrial systems do not supply a simple small-farm lifecycle. |
| Bacalhau | Salted product category commonly involving Gadus species and others. Obtain species and processing identity. |
| Cação | Shark market category; obtain the species and legal provenance. Do not invent a shark-farming lesson. |
| Camarão Rio Grande | Confirm marine shrimp species and wild/farmed origin. Do not silently substitute `@penaeus-vannamei`. |
| Caranguejo | Several crab species with distinct marine/estuarine larval stages and harvest management. Identify before any husbandry plan. |
| Corimba, corimbatá | Prochilodus species depend on basin. @prochilodus-lineatus is one example. |
| Corvina de água doce | Often Plagioscion species; verify. Feeding, basin, and reproduction differ from marine corvinas. |
| Corvina do mar, corvina grande | Several marine sciaenids. Require species and fishery origin. |
| Dourada de água doce | A migratory river catfish name, often Brachyplatystoma rousseauxii; verify rather than substituting dourado. |
| Lambari | Several small characids. Some have aquaculture systems, but stocking and reproduction require the actual species. |
| Merluza | Merluccius and other market identities; generally marine capture. Identify before describing the lifecycle. |
| Pescada, pescadinha | Several marine or freshwater species. The same feeding and breeding advice cannot apply to all. |
| Pintado | Verify @pseudoplatystoma-corruscans, another species, or a declared hybrid. |
| Porquinho | Several fish market names; not the livestock pig. Confirm scientific identity and origin. |
| Salmão | Confirm Atlantic or Pacific species and wild/farmed origin; only explicit Atlantic entries map directly to `@salmo-salar`. |
| Sardinha | Several species, including Brazilian Sardinella and Atlantic `@sardina-pilchardus`. Keep source measurements distinct. |
| Tucunaré | Cichla species, basin-dependent. Predatory life history and reproduction do not match tilapia. |
| Tilápia | Several Oreochromis species and hybrids. @oreochromis-niloticus is the production example, not a proven identity for every fillet. |

Practice: ask a supplier for scientific name, wild/farmed origin, production site or fishery, harvest date, and cold-chain record. Keep uncertain food names linked here until evidence identifies them; do not infer species from a photograph of a cooked portion.
} r_6S6P749VM0QP2X6XZDQ6QGTKM7
